  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 545.492, 558.002, 586.409 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Table 3: Rendering times for our algorithm, with the cost of the diﬀerent steps. The ﬁrst 7 lines are for the Facade scene (2800 triangles), for several projection methods: GL (standard GLSL rendering with per pixel lighting), Sx is Spherical map; Px is Parabola map; xT uses triangles enclosing shape; while xQ uses quad bounding box. The next three lines are for Facade with diﬀerent scene complexity for the PQ algorithm. The last two lines are for larger scenes."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H1",
    "id" : 5,
    "level" : "Doctitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 511.549, 253.822, 525.814 ],
    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "NimbusSanL-Bold",
    "font size" : 11.357,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "6 Conclusion and Future Directions"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 451.849, 294.048, 502.505 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In this paper, we have presented a robust algorithm for handling speciﬁc non-linear projections inside the graphics pipeline. Our algorithm works both for direct display of the non-linear projection, e.g. a ﬁsh-eye lens inside a video game, or for indirect use, e.g. when rendering a shadow map with a paraboloid projection."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 345.404, 294.048, 445.873 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "As with previous work, we start by bouding the projection of each shape, then discard extra fragments inside the bounding shape. Our contributions are twofold. First: two diﬀerent methods for bounding the non-linear projections, one based on triangles that is optimal in fragments but requires more work in the geometry engine, the other based on quads that is optimal for the geometry engine but can causes more overdraw. Second: a mathematical analysis of several non-linear projection methods, where we show that some of them have simple expressions, and thus lend themselves to easy bounding through geometric tools."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 298.734, 294.048, 339.427 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Although non-linear projections are slower than linear projections, the extra cost is manageable. As a single non-linear projection can replace up to ﬁve linear projections (in a hemicube), it can even be a practical alternative, both for rendering time and memory cost."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H1",
    "id" : 9,
    "level" : "Subtitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 272.966, 160.373, 287.231 ],
    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "NimbusSanL-Bold",
    "font size" : 11.357,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Acknowledgements"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.0, 220.394, 294.048, 263.922 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 8.966,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The authors wish to thank the anonymous reviewers for their valuable comments. Nicolas Holzschuch is currently on a sabbatical at Cornell University, funded by the INRIA."
  }, {
